## Orchestrator / Primary lifecycle

A primary agent owns its session from boot to shutdown. This is the Orchestrator
that plans an epic and dispatches workers, or any agent running directly in a
chat window.

### Phase 1 — Boot

The shell/runtime owns `HOLLY_SESSION_ID`. Holly hooks and CLI commands treat it
as read-only identity: they may validate it, record session metadata for it, and
refresh activity, but they do not mint, export, repair, or backfill it.

| Scenario                                          | What happens                                      |
| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |
| Fresh chat window, no `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID`         | BLOCKED: fix shell/runtime bootstrap first        |
| `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` set, session active + fresh   | Updates `last_activity` for the existing identity |
| `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` set, lifecycle ended or stale | Reactivates context for the same identity         |
| `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` set, not in file              | Records metadata for that existing identity       |

When `SessionStart` sees a valid session with no active task, it also creates
and affirms a lightweight intent-only task:
`<agent name> <session id> online and ready.` This unblocks read-only startup
tools without replacing a real issue/worktree task.

Interactive shell startup may assign a terminal-local `HOLLY_SESSION_ID` so
concurrent VS Code/Codex terminals do not all share one identity. Re-sourcing
the shell profile inside the same terminal should keep the existing value.

**After boot, you MUST have a valid `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` in your environment.** Every subsequent tool call depends on it.

```bash
echo $HOLLY_SESSION_ID
# Should print: agent-<uuid> (or an existing sess-XXXXXX)
```

If empty, stop immediately. Do not use Holly lifecycle commands or command
substitution to manufacture an ID. Fix the shell/runtime bootstrap, then
resume.

If the check happens inside context-mode, the sandbox may not inherit the
parent shell's environment. Every `holly ...` command sent through
`ctx_execute` or `ctx_batch_execute` MUST be prefixed:
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID=$HOLLY_SESSION_ID holly ...`. Do not report
`HOLLY_SESSION_ID is not set` from a context-mode result until the exact command
has been retried with that prefix. If the prefix expands empty inside
context-mode, resolve the existing value in the real shell and pass it
explicitly as `HOLLY_SESSION_ID=agent-<existing-parent-shell-value> holly ...`.

In VS Code, booting the session is necessary but not sufficient for gated tools.
To unblock writes and most terminal commands, finish the `agent-sessions`
bootstrap as well: claim the VS Code UUID, then register task state and post
intent with `holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>'` — that
single command also unblocks the gated tools.

### Phase 2 — Claim work and set the task

List unblocked work via the active tracker adapter, then register task context
and post intent in one step:

```bash
holly tracker ready --limit 10
# Records the task, posts intent to all-agents, and unblocks gated tools.
# Later prompts are classified automatically; use resume to re-enter/recover.
eval "$(holly task start --id <issue-id> --intent '<agent-intent>')"
```

**Worktree isolation:** all implementation work happens in a dedicated worktree.
The `pre_tool_use` hook BLOCKS file writes on the main checkout and prints the
exact command to create one through the active adapter. `holly task start`
records task/intent state; it does not itself create the worktree. See
`docs/worktree-workflow.md` for the full lifecycle, or the `issue-tracker`
skill for adapter-specific commands.

### Phase 3 — Deliver (dispatch + integrate)

The `PostToolUse` hook fires on **every** tool call and runs `update_activity()`,
keeping `last_activity` fresh so staleness sweeps don't reap a live session. You
do not need to do anything for this — it just works.

For multi-agent epics, the Orchestrator decomposes the work and dispatches
**bounded workers wave by wave** via `runSubagent` / `Agent`. It collects each
worker's returned report, dispatches a reviewer per branch, and integrates
approved branches in dependency order. **Only the Orchestrator integrates and
closes issues.** See the `swarm-delivery` and `orchestration` skills for the
dispatch contract and routing.

**Human-visible status (optional).** When a human needs to follow progress, a
primary / Orchestrator agent MAY post short ASCII status via the `agent-chat`
skill. This is for humans, not for coordinating workers — swarm dispatch itself
does not use chat. Keep messages ASCII-only and prefer the file-based protocol
(write the body to an `agent-fs` scratch path, then `holly chat post --file`).

### Phase 4 — Idle

When you have no active work and are waiting for the next user prompt, the
session simply goes quiet. The Stop hook marks it `idle` (see Phase 5); the same
`$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` resumes on the next prompt — there is nothing to poll.

> **Legacy note.** The pre-swarm model kept primary agents in a `chat monitor` /
> `holly chat wait --timeout 0` polling loop waiting to be summoned over chat. The
> swarm model does not: workers are dispatched directly by the Orchestrator, so
> there is no standby polling loop to run.

### Phase 5 — Shutdown

The `Stop` hook fires automatically and runs:

1. `idle_session()` — marks the session as `idle` in `.holly/agent-sessions.json`
   (the session is NOT terminated; the same `$HOLLY_SESSION_ID` can be reactivated
   by the next user prompt).
2. The hook does NOT sync the issue tracker. If you want a sync on stop, use
   the active adapter's policy. Adapter workflows may also add their own
   postflight (see `workflows/end-session.yaml`).

The distinction matters:

- `idle` — set by the Stop hook; the window is quiet but the session is
  preserved. Coming back in the same window resumes it.
- `ended` — set explicitly by `holly session end`; the session is closed and
  `ended_at` is stamped. Use this on intentional shutdown.

**Before shutdown, you MUST:**

1. Push all committed work (the feature branch push is mandatory — it is the only backup outside the throwaway worktree).
2. File issues for remaining work via your tracker.
3. Close completed work via your tracker.

**Manual shutdown (if the hook won't fire or you want a hard end):**

```bash
HOLLY_SESSION_ID="$HOLLY_SESSION_ID" holly session end
holly tracker sync  # only when your active adapter requires manual sync
```

